      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The argument that centralized museums offer superior scientific study is a convenient smokescreen that prioritizes Western academic convenience over the sovereignty and cultural integrity of source nations. True scientific understanding requires context, and removing artifacts from their original environments to fit Western curatorial standards actually distorts the very history researchers claim to study. You can argue that \"cross-cultural comparison\" is a myth because it forces disparate cultures into a single Western framework, effectively erasing local narratives in the name of \"universal\" knowledge. Ultimately, the claim of scientific necessity is just another layer of colonial logic that justifies permanent retention rather than repatriation.",
      "source": "target",
